{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "escalint",
          "version": "dev",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/escalint",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "EL-PARSE-001",
              "name": "PolicyDocumentCouldNotBeParsed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Policy document could not be parsed"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Policy document could not be parsed. Fix the policy so it is valid IAM JSON (AWS itself rejects most of these documents); until then, paths that depend on it are not analyzed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/escalint/blob/main/docs/rules/EL-PARSE-001.md",
              "help": {
                "text": "Policy document could not be parsed\n\nRemediation: Fix the policy so it is valid IAM JSON (AWS itself rejects most of these documents); until then, paths that depend on it are not analyzed.",
                "markdown": "# EL-PARSE-001 — Policy document could not be parsed\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Severity | info |\n| Category | parse |\n| Status | stable |\n\n## Description\n\nA policy in the snapshot is not valid IAM policy JSON: invalid JSON, a\nduplicate key, an unknown element, `null` where a value is required, an\nempty list, `NotPrincipal` with `Allow`, and similar cases (ADR 0023). The\nfinding names the policy and the location inside it.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nescalint never guesses what a broken policy means. The policy contributes\nnothing to the analysis, so an escalation path that depends on one of its\n`Allow` statements may be missing, and a `Deny` in it is not applied. AWS\nrejects most of these documents, so a finding usually means the snapshot was\nedited or produced by a tool with a bug.\n\n## Attack chain\n\nNot an attack by itself. A tampered snapshot could try to hide a path with\nan ambiguous document (for example two `Effect` keys); escalint reports it\ninstead of reading it differently from AWS.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```json\n{\"Version\": \"2012-10-17\", \"Statement\": {\"Effect\": \"Deny\", \"Effect\": \"Allow\", \"Action\": \"*\", \"Resource\": \"*\"}}\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```json\n{\"Version\": \"2012-10-17\", \"Statement\": {\"Effect\": \"Deny\", \"Action\": \"*\", \"Resource\": \"*\"}}\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_grammar.html\n- docs/adr/0023-strict-json-for-untrusted-input.md\n"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "high",
                "problem.severity": "note",
                "security-severity": "0.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "iam",
                  "parse"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "EL-PARSE-002",
              "name": "ReferencedPolicyOrGroupIsNotInTheSnapshot",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Referenced policy or group is not in the snapshot"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Referenced policy or group is not in the snapshot. Collect the snapshot again including AWS managed policies (get-account-authorization-details without filters); decisions that depend on the missing policy are conditional."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/escalint/blob/main/docs/rules/EL-PARSE-002.md",
              "help": {
                "text": "Referenced policy or group is not in the snapshot\n\nRemediation: Collect the snapshot again including AWS managed policies (get-account-authorization-details without filters); decisions that depend on the missing policy are conditional.",
                "markdown": "# EL-PARSE-002 — Referenced policy or group is not in the snapshot\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Severity | info |\n| Category | parse |\n| Status | stable |\n\n## Description\n\nA user, group or role references a managed policy, a permissions boundary\nor a group that the snapshot does not contain. This happens when\nauthorization details were collected with a filter (for example without\n`AWSManagedPolicy`).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe permissions of the missing policy are unknown, so every decision that\ndepends on it is `conditional`: it might allow, or deny, what the rest of\nthe policies say.\n\n## Attack chain\n\nNot an attack by itself; it reduces what the analysis can prove.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```json\n{\"UserDetailList\": [{\"UserName\": \"alice\", \"AttachedManagedPolicies\": [{\"PolicyArn\": \"arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/ReadOnlyAccess\"}]}], \"Policies\": []}\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```json\n{\"UserDetailList\": [{\"UserName\": \"alice\", \"AttachedManagedPolicies\": [{\"PolicyArn\": \"arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/ReadOnlyAccess\"}]}],\n \"Policies\": [{\"Arn\": \"arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/ReadOnlyAccess\", \"DefaultVersionId\": \"v1\", \"PolicyVersionList\": [\"…\"]}]}\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/APIReference/API_GetAccountAuthorizationDetails.html\n"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "high",
                "problem.severity": "note",
                "security-severity": "0.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "iam",
                  "parse"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "EL-PARSE-003",
              "name": "SuppressionWithoutAJustification",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Suppression without a justification"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Suppression without a justification. Add a reason to the suppression in .escalint.yaml (who accepted the risk, why, and a ticket), and preferably an expiry date; until then the findings it matches are reported."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/escalint/blob/main/docs/rules/EL-PARSE-003.md",
              "help": {
                "text": "Suppression without a justification\n\nRemediation: Add a reason to the suppression in .escalint.yaml (who accepted the risk, why, and a ticket), and preferably an expiry date; until then the findings it matches are reported.",
                "markdown": "# EL-PARSE-003 — Suppression without a justification\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Severity | info |\n| Category | parse |\n| Status | stable |\n\n## Description\n\nA suppression in `.escalint.yaml` has an empty `reason`. escalint requires a\njustification for every suppression: without one, the suppression is\nrecorded with status `unjustified`, suppresses nothing, and this finding\npoints at it.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nSuppressions hide real escalation paths from reports and CI gates. A reason\n(who accepted the risk, why, and where it was discussed) keeps that decision\nreviewable; an expiry date makes sure it is reviewed again.\n\n## Attack chain\n\nNot an attack by itself; it keeps accepted risks visible and accountable.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```yaml\nversion: 1\nsuppressions:\n  - rule: EL-TR-004\n    arn: \"arn:aws:iam::111111111111:role/legacy-partner\"\n    reason: \"\"\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```yaml\nversion: 1\nsuppressions:\n  - rule: EL-TR-004\n    arn: \"arn:aws:iam::111111111111:role/legacy-partner\"\n    reason: \"Partner integration approved by the security team (SEC-1234)\"\n    expires: 2027-01-31\n```\n\n## References\n\n- https://github.com/brennocm/escalint/blob/main/docs/configuration.md\n"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "high",
                "problem.severity": "note",
                "security-severity": "0.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "iam",
                  "parse"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "EL-PE-001",
              "name": "CreateANewDefaultVersionOfAnAttachedPolicy",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Create a new default version of an attached policy"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Create a new default version of an attached policy. Remove iam:CreatePolicyVersion or scope it to policies that are not attached to the caller; deny it in a permissions boundary."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/escalint/blob/main/docs/rules/EL-PE-001.md",
              "help": {
                "text": "Create a new default version of an attached policy\n\nRemediation: Remove iam:CreatePolicyVersion or scope it to policies that are not attached to the caller; deny it in a permissions boundary.",
                "markdown": "# EL-PE-001 — Create a new default version of an attached policy\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Severity | critical (critical when the edge is on a path to Tier 0) |\n| Category | self-escalation |\n| Status | stable |\n| Edge | `modify_permissions` |\n| Required actions | `iam:CreatePolicyVersion` |\n| pathfinding.cloud | [iam-001](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-001) |\n\n## Description\n\nThe principal can change its own permissions. Nothing else is needed to become an administrator of the account.\n\nPrerequisites:\n\n- a customer managed policy is attached to the actor (directly or through a group)\n\n## Why it matters\n\nEscalation paths turn a limited identity into an administrator. Attackers who obtain any credential look for exactly this permission combination; defenders rarely notice it in policy reviews because each statement looks reasonable on its own.\n\n## Attack chain\n\n1. The principal calls `iam:CreatePolicyVersion` on itself (or on a policy/group it already uses) with an administrative policy document.\n2. The new permissions apply immediately: the principal is now an effective administrator.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:CreatePolicyVersion\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:iam::111111111111:policy/AppPolicy\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:CreatePolicyVersion\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:iam::111111111111:role/app-workers/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Remediation\n\nRemove iam:CreatePolicyVersion or scope it to policies that are not attached to the caller; deny it in a permissions boundary.\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/APIReference/API_CreatePolicyVersion.html\n- https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/269.html\n- pathfinding.cloud: [iam-001](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-001)\n"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "high",
                "problem.severity": "error",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "iam",
                  "self-escalation",
                  "external/attack/t1098.003",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-269"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "EL-PE-002",
              "name": "PutAnInlinePolicyOnItsOwnRole",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Put an inline policy on its own role"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Put an inline policy on its own role. Remove iam:PutRolePolicy from the role's policies, or scope its Resource to roles other than itself and add a permissions boundary that denies it."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/escalint/blob/main/docs/rules/EL-PE-002.md",
              "help": {
                "text": "Put an inline policy on its own role\n\nRemediation: Remove iam:PutRolePolicy from the role's policies, or scope its Resource to roles other than itself and add a permissions boundary that denies it.",
                "markdown": "# EL-PE-002 — Put an inline policy on its own role\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Severity | critical (critical when the edge is on a path to Tier 0) |\n| Category | self-escalation |\n| Status | stable |\n| Edge | `modify_permissions` |\n| Required actions | `iam:PutRolePolicy` |\n| pathfinding.cloud | [iam-005](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-005) |\n\n## Description\n\nThe principal can change its own permissions. Nothing else is needed to become an administrator of the account.\n\nPrerequisites:\n\n- the actor is a role allowed iam:PutRolePolicy on its own ARN\n\n## Why it matters\n\nEscalation paths turn a limited identity into an administrator. Attackers who obtain any credential look for exactly this permission combination; defenders rarely notice it in policy reviews because each statement looks reasonable on its own.\n\n## Attack chain\n\n1. The principal calls `iam:PutRolePolicy` on itself (or on a policy/group it already uses) with an administrative policy document.\n2. The new permissions apply immediately: the principal is now an effective administrator.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:PutRolePolicy\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:PutRolePolicy\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:iam::111111111111:role/app-workers/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Remediation\n\nRemove iam:PutRolePolicy from the role's policies, or scope its Resource to roles other than itself and add a permissions boundary that denies it.\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/APIReference/API_PutRolePolicy.html\n- https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/269.html\n- pathfinding.cloud: [iam-005](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-005)\n"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "high",
                "problem.severity": "error",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "iam",
                  "self-escalation",
                  "external/attack/t1098.003",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-269"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "EL-PE-003",
              "name": "PutAnInlinePolicyOnItsOwnUser",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Put an inline policy on its own user"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Put an inline policy on its own user. Remove iam:PutUserPolicy, or limit it with Resource and a permissions boundary so users cannot target themselves."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/escalint/blob/main/docs/rules/EL-PE-003.md",
              "help": {
                "text": "Put an inline policy on its own user\n\nRemediation: Remove iam:PutUserPolicy, or limit it with Resource and a permissions boundary so users cannot target themselves.",
                "markdown": "# EL-PE-003 — Put an inline policy on its own user\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Severity | critical (critical when the edge is on a path to Tier 0) |\n| Category | self-escalation |\n| Status | stable |\n| Edge | `modify_permissions` |\n| Required actions | `iam:PutUserPolicy` |\n| pathfinding.cloud | [iam-007](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-007) |\n\n## Description\n\nThe principal can change its own permissions. Nothing else is needed to become an administrator of the account.\n\nPrerequisites:\n\n- the actor is a user allowed iam:PutUserPolicy on its own ARN\n\n## Why it matters\n\nEscalation paths turn a limited identity into an administrator. Attackers who obtain any credential look for exactly this permission combination; defenders rarely notice it in policy reviews because each statement looks reasonable on its own.\n\n## Attack chain\n\n1. The principal calls `iam:PutUserPolicy` on itself (or on a policy/group it already uses) with an administrative policy document.\n2. The new permissions apply immediately: the principal is now an effective administrator.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:PutUserPolicy\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:PutUserPolicy\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:iam::111111111111:role/app-workers/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Remediation\n\nRemove iam:PutUserPolicy, or limit it with Resource and a permissions boundary so users cannot target themselves.\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/APIReference/API_PutUserPolicy.html\n- https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/269.html\n- pathfinding.cloud: [iam-007](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-007)\n"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "high",
                "problem.severity": "error",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "iam",
                  "self-escalation",
                  "external/attack/t1098.003",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-269"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "EL-PE-004",
              "name": "AttachAManagedPolicyToItsOwnUser",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Attach a managed policy to its own user"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Attach a managed policy to its own user. Remove the action from the principal's policies, scope its Resource to other entities, or add a permissions boundary that denies it. A condition on iam:PolicyARN can limit which policies may be attached."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/escalint/blob/main/docs/rules/EL-PE-004.md",
              "help": {
                "text": "Attach a managed policy to its own user\n\nRemediation: Remove the action from the principal's policies, scope its Resource to other entities, or add a permissions boundary that denies it. A condition on iam:PolicyARN can limit which policies may be attached.",
                "markdown": "# EL-PE-004 — Attach a managed policy to its own user\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Severity | critical (critical when the edge is on a path to Tier 0) |\n| Category | self-escalation |\n| Status | stable |\n| Edge | `modify_permissions` |\n| Required actions | `iam:AttachUserPolicy` |\n| pathfinding.cloud | [iam-008](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-008) |\n\n## Description\n\nThe principal can change its own permissions. Nothing else is needed to become an administrator of the account.\n\nPrerequisites:\n\n- the actor is a user\n\n## Why it matters\n\nEscalation paths turn a limited identity into an administrator. Attackers who obtain any credential look for exactly this permission combination; defenders rarely notice it in policy reviews because each statement looks reasonable on its own.\n\n## Attack chain\n\n1. The principal calls `iam:AttachUserPolicy` on itself (or on a policy/group it already uses) with an administrative policy document.\n2. The new permissions apply immediately: the principal is now an effective administrator.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:AttachUserPolicy\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:AttachUserPolicy\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:iam::111111111111:role/app-workers/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Remediation\n\nRemove the action from the principal's policies, scope its Resource to other entities, or add a permissions boundary that denies it. A condition on iam:PolicyARN can limit which policies may be attached.\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/APIReference/API_AttachUserPolicy.html\n- https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/269.html\n- pathfinding.cloud: [iam-008](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-008)\n"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "high",
                "problem.severity": "error",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "iam",
                  "self-escalation",
                  "external/attack/t1098.003",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-269"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "EL-PE-005",
              "name": "AttachAManagedPolicyToItsOwnRole",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Attach a managed policy to its own role"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Attach a managed policy to its own role. Remove the action from the principal's policies, scope its Resource to other entities, or add a permissions boundary that denies it. A condition on iam:PolicyARN can limit which policies may be attached."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/escalint/blob/main/docs/rules/EL-PE-005.md",
              "help": {
                "text": "Attach a managed policy to its own role\n\nRemediation: Remove the action from the principal's policies, scope its Resource to other entities, or add a permissions boundary that denies it. A condition on iam:PolicyARN can limit which policies may be attached.",
                "markdown": "# EL-PE-005 — Attach a managed policy to its own role\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Severity | critical (critical when the edge is on a path to Tier 0) |\n| Category | self-escalation |\n| Status | stable |\n| Edge | `modify_permissions` |\n| Required actions | `iam:AttachRolePolicy` |\n| pathfinding.cloud | [iam-009](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-009) |\n\n## Description\n\nThe principal can change its own permissions. Nothing else is needed to become an administrator of the account.\n\nPrerequisites:\n\n- the actor is a role\n\n## Why it matters\n\nEscalation paths turn a limited identity into an administrator. Attackers who obtain any credential look for exactly this permission combination; defenders rarely notice it in policy reviews because each statement looks reasonable on its own.\n\n## Attack chain\n\n1. The principal calls `iam:AttachRolePolicy` on itself (or on a policy/group it already uses) with an administrative policy document.\n2. The new permissions apply immediately: the principal is now an effective administrator.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:AttachRolePolicy\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:AttachRolePolicy\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:iam::111111111111:role/app-workers/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Remediation\n\nRemove the action from the principal's policies, scope its Resource to other entities, or add a permissions boundary that denies it. A condition on iam:PolicyARN can limit which policies may be attached.\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/APIReference/API_AttachRolePolicy.html\n- https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/269.html\n- pathfinding.cloud: [iam-009](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-009)\n"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "high",
                "problem.severity": "error",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "iam",
                  "self-escalation",
                  "external/attack/t1098.003",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-269"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "EL-PE-006",
              "name": "AttachAManagedPolicyToItsOwnGroup",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Attach a managed policy to its own group"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Attach a managed policy to its own group. Remove the action from the principal's policies, scope its Resource to other entities, or add a permissions boundary that denies it."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/escalint/blob/main/docs/rules/EL-PE-006.md",
              "help": {
                "text": "Attach a managed policy to its own group\n\nRemediation: Remove the action from the principal's policies, scope its Resource to other entities, or add a permissions boundary that denies it.",
                "markdown": "# EL-PE-006 — Attach a managed policy to its own group\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Severity | critical (critical when the edge is on a path to Tier 0) |\n| Category | self-escalation |\n| Status | stable |\n| Edge | `modify_permissions` |\n| Required actions | `iam:AttachGroupPolicy` |\n| pathfinding.cloud | [iam-010](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-010) |\n\n## Description\n\nThe principal can change its own permissions. Nothing else is needed to become an administrator of the account.\n\nPrerequisites:\n\n- the actor is a member of the group\n\n## Why it matters\n\nEscalation paths turn a limited identity into an administrator. Attackers who obtain any credential look for exactly this permission combination; defenders rarely notice it in policy reviews because each statement looks reasonable on its own.\n\n## Attack chain\n\n1. The principal calls `iam:AttachGroupPolicy` on itself (or on a policy/group it already uses) with an administrative policy document.\n2. The new permissions apply immediately: the principal is now an effective administrator.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:AttachGroupPolicy\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:iam::111111111111:group/developers\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:AttachGroupPolicy\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:iam::111111111111:role/app-workers/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Remediation\n\nRemove the action from the principal's policies, scope its Resource to other entities, or add a permissions boundary that denies it.\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/APIReference/API_AttachGroupPolicy.html\n- https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/269.html\n- pathfinding.cloud: [iam-010](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-010)\n"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "high",
                "problem.severity": "error",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "iam",
                  "self-escalation",
                  "external/attack/t1098.003",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-269"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "EL-PE-007",
              "name": "PutAnInlinePolicyOnItsOwnGroup",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Put an inline policy on its own group"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Put an inline policy on its own group. Remove the action from the principal's policies, scope its Resource to other entities, or add a permissions boundary that denies it."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/escalint/blob/main/docs/rules/EL-PE-007.md",
              "help": {
                "text": "Put an inline policy on its own group\n\nRemediation: Remove the action from the principal's policies, scope its Resource to other entities, or add a permissions boundary that denies it.",
                "markdown": "# EL-PE-007 — Put an inline policy on its own group\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Severity | critical (critical when the edge is on a path to Tier 0) |\n| Category | self-escalation |\n| Status | stable |\n| Edge | `modify_permissions` |\n| Required actions | `iam:PutGroupPolicy` |\n| pathfinding.cloud | [iam-011](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-011) |\n\n## Description\n\nThe principal can change its own permissions. Nothing else is needed to become an administrator of the account.\n\nPrerequisites:\n\n- the actor is a member of the group\n\n## Why it matters\n\nEscalation paths turn a limited identity into an administrator. Attackers who obtain any credential look for exactly this permission combination; defenders rarely notice it in policy reviews because each statement looks reasonable on its own.\n\n## Attack chain\n\n1. The principal calls `iam:PutGroupPolicy` on itself (or on a policy/group it already uses) with an administrative policy document.\n2. The new permissions apply immediately: the principal is now an effective administrator.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:PutGroupPolicy\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:iam::111111111111:group/developers\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:PutGroupPolicy\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:iam::111111111111:role/app-workers/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Remediation\n\nRemove the action from the principal's policies, scope its Resource to other entities, or add a permissions boundary that denies it.\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/APIReference/API_PutGroupPolicy.html\n- https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/269.html\n- pathfinding.cloud: [iam-011](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-011)\n"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "high",
                "problem.severity": "error",
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "iam",
                  "self-escalation",
                  "external/attack/t1098.003",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-269"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "EL-PE-008",
              "name": "AddItselfToAMorePrivilegedGroup",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Add itself to a more privileged group"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Add itself to a more privileged group. Scope iam:AddUserToGroup to specific groups and never allow it on groups with administrative policies."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/escalint/blob/main/docs/rules/EL-PE-008.md",
              "help": {
                "text": "Add itself to a more privileged group\n\nRemediation: Scope iam:AddUserToGroup to specific groups and never allow it on groups with administrative policies.",
                "markdown": "# EL-PE-008 — Add itself to a more privileged group\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Severity | high (critical when the edge is on a path to Tier 0) |\n| Category | self-escalation |\n| Status | stable |\n| Edge | `modify_permissions` |\n| Required actions | `iam:AddUserToGroup` |\n| pathfinding.cloud | [iam-013](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-013) |\n\n## Description\n\nThe principal can take over another IAM principal by acting on its credentials, trust policy or group membership.\n\nPrerequisites:\n\n- the actor is a user that is not yet a member\n\n## Why it matters\n\nEscalation paths turn a limited identity into an administrator. Attackers who obtain any credential look for exactly this permission combination; defenders rarely notice it in policy reviews because each statement looks reasonable on its own.\n\n## Attack chain\n\n1. The user calls `iam:AddUserToGroup` to join `arn:aws:iam::111111111111:group/admins`.\n2. The group's policies apply to the user immediately.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:AddUserToGroup\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:AddUserToGroup\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:iam::111111111111:group/team-a/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Remediation\n\nScope iam:AddUserToGroup to specific groups and never allow it on groups with administrative policies.\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/APIReference/API_AddUserToGroup.html\n- https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/269.html\n- pathfinding.cloud: [iam-013](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-013)\n"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "high",
                "problem.severity": "error",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "iam",
                  "self-escalation",
                  "external/attack/t1098.003",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-269"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "EL-PE-009",
              "name": "CreateAnAccessKeyForAnotherUser",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Create an access key for another user"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Create an access key for another user. Scope iam:CreateAccessKey to the caller's own user (arn:aws:iam::\u003caccount\u003e:user/${aws:username})."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/escalint/blob/main/docs/rules/EL-PE-009.md",
              "help": {
                "text": "Create an access key for another user\n\nRemediation: Scope iam:CreateAccessKey to the caller's own user (arn:aws:iam::\u003caccount\u003e:user/${aws:username}).",
                "markdown": "# EL-PE-009 — Create an access key for another user\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Severity | high (critical when the edge is on a path to Tier 0) |\n| Category | principal-access |\n| Status | stable |\n| Edge | `credential` |\n| Required actions | `iam:CreateAccessKey` |\n| pathfinding.cloud | [iam-002](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-002) |\n\n## Description\n\nThe principal can take over another IAM principal by acting on its credentials, trust policy or group membership.\n\nPrerequisites:\n\n- the target user has fewer than 2 access keys\n\n## Why it matters\n\nEscalation paths turn a limited identity into an administrator. Attackers who obtain any credential look for exactly this permission combination; defenders rarely notice it in policy reviews because each statement looks reasonable on its own.\n\n## Attack chain\n\n1. The principal calls `iam:CreateAccessKey` for `arn:aws:iam::111111111111:user/target`.\n2. It signs in or calls AWS as that user, with the user's permissions.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:CreateAccessKey\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:CreateAccessKey\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:iam::111111111111:user/${aws:username}\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Remediation\n\nScope iam:CreateAccessKey to the caller's own user (arn:aws:iam::\u003caccount\u003e:user/${aws:username}).\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/APIReference/API_CreateAccessKey.html\n- https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/269.html\n- pathfinding.cloud: [iam-002](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-002)\n"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "high",
                "problem.severity": "error",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "iam",
                  "principal-access",
                  "external/attack/t1098.001",
                  "external/attack/t1078.004",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-269"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "EL-PE-010",
              "name": "RotateOutAndCreateAnAccessKeyForAnotherUser",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Rotate out and create an access key for another user"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Rotate out and create an access key for another user. Scope iam:CreateAccessKey and iam:DeleteAccessKey to the caller's own user (arn:aws:iam::\u003caccount\u003e:user/${aws:username})."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/escalint/blob/main/docs/rules/EL-PE-010.md",
              "help": {
                "text": "Rotate out and create an access key for another user\n\nRemediation: Scope iam:CreateAccessKey and iam:DeleteAccessKey to the caller's own user (arn:aws:iam::\u003caccount\u003e:user/${aws:username}).",
                "markdown": "# EL-PE-010 — Rotate out and create an access key for another user\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Severity | high (critical when the edge is on a path to Tier 0) |\n| Category | principal-access |\n| Status | stable |\n| Edge | `credential` |\n| Required actions | `iam:CreateAccessKey`, `iam:DeleteAccessKey` |\n| pathfinding.cloud | [iam-003](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-003) |\n\n## Description\n\nThe principal can take over another IAM principal by acting on its credentials, trust policy or group membership.\n\nPrerequisites:\n\n- the target is an IAM user\n\n## Why it matters\n\nEscalation paths turn a limited identity into an administrator. Attackers who obtain any credential look for exactly this permission combination; defenders rarely notice it in policy reviews because each statement looks reasonable on its own.\n\n## Attack chain\n\n1. The principal calls `iam:CreateAccessKey` for `arn:aws:iam::111111111111:user/target`.\n2. It signs in or calls AWS as that user, with the user's permissions.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:CreateAccessKey\",\n        \"iam:DeleteAccessKey\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:CreateAccessKey\",\n        \"iam:DeleteAccessKey\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:iam::111111111111:user/${aws:username}\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Remediation\n\nScope iam:CreateAccessKey and iam:DeleteAccessKey to the caller's own user (arn:aws:iam::\u003caccount\u003e:user/${aws:username}).\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/APIReference/API_CreateAccessKey.html\n- https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/269.html\n- pathfinding.cloud: [iam-003](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-003)\n"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "high",
