            {
              "id": "EL-PE-014",
              "name": "AssumeAMorePrivilegedRole",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Assume a more privileged role"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Assume a more privileged role. Name only the principals that need the role in its trust policy, add conditions (sts:ExternalId, aws:PrincipalOrgID, MFA) and scope sts:AssumeRole in identity policies to specific role ARNs."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/escalint/blob/main/docs/rules/EL-PE-014.md",
              "help": {
                "text": "Assume a more privileged role\n\nRemediation: Name only the principals that need the role in its trust policy, add conditions (sts:ExternalId, aws:PrincipalOrgID, MFA) and scope sts:AssumeRole in identity policies to specific role ARNs.",
                "markdown": "# EL-PE-014 — Assume a more privileged role\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Severity | medium (critical when the edge is on a path to Tier 0) |\n| Category | principal-access |\n| Status | stable |\n| Edge | `assume` |\n| Required actions | `sts:AssumeRole` |\n| pathfinding.cloud | [sts-001](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/sts-001) |\n\n## Description\n\nThe principal can assume a role whose permissions exceed its own.\n\nPrerequisites:\n\n- the role's trust policy accepts the actor (docs/research.md §2.4)\n\n## Why it matters\n\nEscalation paths turn a limited identity into an administrator. Attackers who obtain any credential look for exactly this permission combination; defenders rarely notice it in policy reviews because each statement looks reasonable on its own.\n\n## Attack chain\n\n1. The principal calls `sts:AssumeRole` on a role whose trust policy accepts it.\n2. It acts with the role's permissions.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Principal\": {\n        \"AWS\": \"arn:aws:iam::111111111111:root\"\n      },\n      \"Action\": \"sts:AssumeRole\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Principal\": {\n        \"AWS\": \"arn:aws:iam::111111111111:role/deployer\"\n      },\n      \"Action\": \"sts:AssumeRole\",\n      \"Condition\": {\n        \"Bool\": {\n          \"aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent\": \"true\"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Remediation\n\nName only the principals that need the role in its trust policy, add conditions (sts:ExternalId, aws:PrincipalOrgID, MFA) and scope sts:AssumeRole in identity policies to specific role ARNs.\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.aws.amazon.com/STS/latest/APIReference/API_AssumeRole.html\n- https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/269.html\n- pathfinding.cloud: [sts-001](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/sts-001)\n"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "high",
                "problem.severity": "warning",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
