            {
              "id": "EL-PE-015",
              "name": "AttachAPolicyToARoleItCanAssume",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Attach a policy to a role it can assume"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Attach a policy to a role it can assume. Remove the permission-management action on other principals, or deny it in a permissions boundary; also review how the caller can act as the target."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/escalint/blob/main/docs/rules/EL-PE-015.md",
              "help": {
                "text": "Attach a policy to a role it can assume\n\nRemediation: Remove the permission-management action on other principals, or deny it in a permissions boundary; also review how the caller can act as the target.",
                "markdown": "# EL-PE-015 — Attach a policy to a role it can assume\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Severity | critical (critical when the edge is on a path to Tier 0) |\n| Category | principal-access |\n| Status | stable |\n| Edge | `modify_permissions` |\n| Required actions | `iam:AttachRolePolicy`, `sts:AssumeRole` |\n| pathfinding.cloud | [iam-014](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/iam-014) |\n\n## Description\n\nThe principal can raise the permissions of another principal and then act as it, turning a partial privilege into administrator access.\n\nPrerequisites:\n\n- the actor can already assume the role\n\n## Why it matters\n\nEscalation paths turn a limited identity into an administrator. Attackers who obtain any credential look for exactly this permission combination; defenders rarely notice it in policy reviews because each statement looks reasonable on its own.\n\n## Attack chain\n\n1. The principal grants administrative permissions to a principal it can become (`iam:AttachRolePolicy`).\n2. It becomes that principal (`sts:AssumeRole`) and acts as an administrator.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"iam:AttachRolePolicy\",\n        \"sts:AssumeRole\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"sts:AssumeRole\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:iam::111111111111:role/team-a/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Remediation\n\nRemove the permission-management action on other principals, or deny it in a permissions boundary; also review how the caller can act as the target.\n\n## References\n\n- https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/APIReference/API_AttachRolePolicy.html\n- https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/269.html\n- pathfinding.cloud: [iam-014](https:/