            {
              "id": "EL-PE-028",
              "name": "UpdateTheCodeOfAnExistingLambdaFunctionAndInvokeIt",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Update the code of an existing Lambda function and invoke it"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Update the code of an existing Lambda function and invoke it. Restrict lambda:UpdateFunctionCode and lambda:InvokeFunction to the resources the caller owns; separate deployment roles from runtime roles."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/brennocm/escalint/blob/main/docs/rules/EL-PE-028.md",
              "help": {
                "text": "Update the code of an existing Lambda function and invoke it\n\nRemediation: Restrict lambda:UpdateFunctionCode and lambda:InvokeFunction to the resources the caller owns; separate deployment roles from runtime roles.",
                "markdown": "# EL-PE-028 — Update the code of an existing Lambda function and invoke it\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Severity | high (critical when the edge is on a path to Tier 0) |\n| Category | existing-passrole |\n| Status | stable |\n| Edge | `update_code` |\n| Required actions | `lambda:UpdateFunctionCode`, `lambda:InvokeFunction` |\n| pathfinding.cloud | [lambda-004](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/lambda-004) |\n\n## Description\n\nThe principal can change what an existing resource runs, and that resource already has a role. The new code runs with the role's permissions.\n\nPrerequisites:\n\n- a Lambda function runs with the role\n\n## Why it matters\n\nEscalation paths turn a limited identity into an administrator. Attackers who obtain any credential look for exactly this permission combination; defenders rarely notice it in policy reviews because each statement looks reasonable on its own.\n\n## Attack chain\n\n1. The principal changes what an existing resource runs (`lambda:UpdateFunctionCode`, `lambda:InvokeFunction`).\n2. The resource executes that code with its role's credentials.\n\n## Vulnerable example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"lambda:UpdateFunctionCode\",\n        \"lambda:InvokeFunction\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Fixed example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"lambda:UpdateFunctionCode\",\n        \"lambda:InvokeFunction\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:lambda:*:111111111111:function:team-a-*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Remediation\n\nRestrict lambda:UpdateFunctionCode and lambda:InvokeFunction to the resources the caller owns; separate deployment roles from runtime roles.\n\n## References\n\n- https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/266.html\n- pathfinding.cloud: [lambda-004](https://pathfinding.cloud/paths/aws/lambda-004)\n"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "high",
                "problem.severity": "error",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "iam",
                  "existing-passrole",
                  "external/attack/t1648",
                  "external/attack/t1548.005",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-266"
                ]
              }
